WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.990 --> 00:00:03.960
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:08.970
NARRATOR: The piñata is a
colorful paper mache object

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:12.210
typically filled with
candy or small toys, then

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:14.870
suspended high from a string.

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:18.170
Blindfolded partiers take
turns trying to hit the piñata

00:00:18.170 --> 00:00:20.930
with a stick to break
it open and release

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:21.965
a shower of goodies.

00:00:27.290 --> 00:00:31.100
From popular cartoon characters
to colorful, glittery shapes,

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:34.160
today's piñatas are a
big hit at kids' parties,

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:36.350
where children eagerly
await the decisive

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:38.720
blow that breaks
open the piñata,

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:40.250
releasing the treats inside.

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:46.190
The traditional way to build
one starts with a clay pot

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:47.375
suspended from a string.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:51.590
The piñata maker
glues cardboard shapes

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:55.190
to it and secures them by
gluing on pieces of newspaper.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:02.150
Then she covers the
rest of the pot.

00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:05.030
When the glue dries, she
decorates the surface

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:06.095
with colorful paper.

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:16.280
The modern method is to use a
balloon instead of a clay pot.

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:23.880
After inflating the balloon,
she pastes pieces of newspaper

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:25.080
all over it.

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:27.195
This technique is
known as paper maché.

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:37.070
The trick is to
completely saturate

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:39.620
the newspaper with a
thick paste and make

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:42.737
sure no part of the
balloon is left uncovered.

00:01:42.737 --> 00:01:45.122
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:52.560
She continues this
process until she's

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:55.290
built up two or three
even layers of newspaper

00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:56.340
all over the balloon.

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:01.620
Then she suspends it from
a clothes pin to dry.

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:12.600
Hours later, the paper mache
is hardened into a shell

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:15.810
around the balloon, which
she now breaks and removes.

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:23.650
Next, she cuts an opening
at the top of the shell.

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:25.470
This is the hole through
which the buyer will

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:27.540
fill the pinata with treats.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:30.450
She'll later reattach the
cut piece to make a trapdoor.

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:36.060
But first, with the sharp
tips of her scissors,

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:39.120
she pierces a row of holes
along the circumference

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:40.950
and threads twine through them.

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:51.440
She leaves long
ends at the top with

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:53.090
which to suspend the piñata.

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:55.510
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:04.140
This piñata will be star-shaped.

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:07.980
To make each point, she rolls
thin cardboard into a cone,

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:08.925
taping it closed.

00:03:15.350 --> 00:03:19.250
She trims it to
the required size,

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:22.250
then cuts tabs at the
base and bends them back.

00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:30.380
She makes seven of
these cardboard points,

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:32.840
then tapes them onto
the shell one by one.

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:35.834
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:45.210
Once all seven are
positioned, the decorating

00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:48.210
with colorful, glittery foil
wrapping paper can begin.

00:03:53.250 --> 00:04:03.240
She tapes a sheet around
each point of the star,

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:06.000
then she applies glue
in between the points

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:08.730
and adheres pieces of
wrapping paper to those areas.

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:15.150
She folds other pieces
into flower shapes

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:16.680
and glues them on as well.

00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:26.570
Once the entire
piñata is covered,

00:04:26.570 --> 00:04:29.405
she tapes matching streamers
to the end of each point.

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:34.370
Historians believe Spanish
missionaries used star piñatas

00:04:34.370 --> 00:04:37.640
like this one to convert the
Native peoples in the Americas

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:39.470
to Christianity.

00:04:39.470 --> 00:04:42.740
The star symbolized
Satan, its seven points

00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:46.910
the seven deadly sins, its
beautiful colors temptation.

00:04:46.910 --> 00:04:50.300
Wearing a blindfold
represented blind faith in God.

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:52.880
Striking the piñata with
a stick meant goodness

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:55.010
and virtue battling evil.

00:04:55.010 --> 00:04:58.790
Breaking the piñata, releasing
a shower of treats or trinkets,

00:04:58.790 --> 00:05:01.250
symbolized heavenly rewards.

00:05:01.250 --> 00:05:04.300
[MUSIC PLAYING]

